ccounted for), there is a significant risk of a Bomb being held by an opponent.\n1. **Play Red Joker first**: It guarantees winning the trick if not bombed. However, if an opponent plays a Bomb on the Red Joker, I will be left with the Ace. With the \'2\' still in play, my Ace is not a guaranteed winner, and I could lose control and the game.\n2. **Play Ace of Hearts first**: \n   - If opponents pass, I play the Red Joker and win.\n   - If an opponent plays the \'2\', I play the Red Joker immediately and win.\n   - If an opponent plays a Bomb on the Ace, I am left with the Red Joker. Since the Black Joker is already played, no Rocket is possible. The Red Joker is unbeatable (except by a higher bomb, which is unlikely given counts). I will almost certainly regain control on the next trick and win.\n\nReason: Playing the Ace first forces the opponents to use their high cards (the 2) or a Bomb. Keeping the Red Joker as the final card (or the response to the 2) ensures I have the ultimate stopper protected. If I play the Joker first and get bombed, I am left vulnerable. Playing the Ace first covers all scenarios safest.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥A"]}'}
